Application-native deterministic results are application-owned.
They bypass external response ingestion.
They use a dedicated validated application command.
They do not require human proposal review.

The application atomically records:
- execution-event identity;
- test ID;
- Test IR hash;
- runtime version;
- input artifact IDs;
- input artifact hashes;
- normalized observations;
- normalized result;
- evidence IDs;
- project revision;
- event sequence;
- canonical receipt.

22.5 External deterministic results

Native and external deterministic outcomes may share the canonical deterministicResults collection only when all gate-consumed canonical fields remain consistently application-owned.

External executors submit:
- observations;
- tool metadata;
- evidence claims;
- returned artifacts.

The application validates and normalizes them into canonical application-owned results.
Producer authority does not change by route.

22.6 Runtime identity and deterministic dataflow evidence

Every native execution result additionally records runtimeBuildIdentity, testWorkerSha256, workerProtocolVersion, TEST_IR_LANGUAGE_VERSION, OPERATION_REGISTRY_VERSION, OPERATION_REGISTRY_SHA256, every parser/selector/regex registry identity actually used, the normalized DAG hash, and the exact output port selected as the result. A result lacking any identity required by its execution path is not release-bearing.

The worker executes only the validated explicit DAG. It cannot infer missing operands, read a nondeclared binding, access a later step, or continue a dependent step after a predecessor error.

28. Prompt context isolation

The actual selected context must match the prompt's stated restrictions.
A warning not to use information is insufficient if the application embeds that information.

28.1 Stage 11 may include
- frozen candidate;
- current reserved run ID;
- current context identity or alias;
- authorized project inputs;
- tool configuration.

It excludes:
- every other run output;
- verification results;
- comparison findings;
- defects discovered after candidate freeze;
- RCA;
- corrections;
- reviewer feedback.

28.2 Stage 12 may include
- exact target run or blind alias;
- target requirement;
- target test;
- required artifacts;
- relevant governing evidence.

It excludes:
- generator self-evaluation;
- other verifier determinations;
- Stage 13 comparison;
- RCA;
- correction proposals.

28.3 Stage 23 may include
- actual product;
- governing requirement;
- applicable source evidence;
- applicable meaning test or rubric.

It excludes:
- generator correctness claims;
- deterministic pass status when unnecessary;
- adversarial findings;
- prior meaning-review conclusions.

28.4 Stage 24 may include
- actual product;
- applicable requirements;
- authorized regressions;
- authorized failure patterns.

It excludes:
- generator reasoning;
- generator self-evaluation;
- conclusions telling the reviewer what to find;
- prior reviewer conclusions unless an explicit attack requires them.

