17.4 Record identity

A proposed record uses exactly one of:
- tempKey for a response-local new proposal;
- targetId for an application-reserved current slot.

A targetId must:
- already exist;
- belong to the same project;
- belong to the correct stage and operation;
- belong to the current scope;
- permit completion or update;
- expose only agent-writable fields.

Humans and agents cannot allocate canonical IDs.

17.5 Closed-schema rejection

The single ingestion path rejects before canonical mutation:
- malformed JSON;
- truncated JSON;
- Markdown-wrapped JSON;
- oversized JSON;
- duplicate member names;
- unsafe smart or curly quotation output;
- wrong root type;
- unknown closed properties;
- wrong job;
- wrong stage;
- wrong operation;
- wrong prompt identity;
- wrong response contract;
- wrong context signature;
- wrong project revision;
- wrong scope;
- stale input version;
- stale source version;
- stale requirements version;
- stale test version;
- stale instruction version;
- stale iteration;
- stale candidate;
- stale run;
- stale baseline;
- stale product;
- ownership violations;
- wrong type;
- wrong enum;
- prohibited null;
- invalid required empty;
- non-finite number;
- unresolved relationships;
- duplicate relationships;
- wrong relationship cardinality;
- cross-project references;
- missing provenance;
- invalid provenance;
- missing required attachment;
- mismatched filename;
- mismatched media type;
- mismatched size;
- mismatched actual-byte hash;
- duplicate semantic response;
- duplicate canonical content;
- target collision;
- stale proposal;
- illegal repeated disposition transition;
- unknown Test IR version;
- unknown Test IR operation;
- invalid operation arguments;
- unsupported or unsafe Test IR construct;
- invalid Test IR binding;
- external override of application-derived independence;
- external override of evidence sufficiency;
- external fabrication of native results;
- external override of release state.

Phase 3 — canonical acceptance
- recompute every precondition;
- reverify every staged byte set;
- atomically promote the exact response and returned-file bytes;
- atomically apply the complete canonical change;
- derive state and invalidate downstream work;
- complete the receipt and extraction manifest.

17.8 Extraction manifest

Every accepted agent value and relationship maps to:
- exact raw response;
- raw-response hash;
- exact JSON pointer with real array index;
- controlling prompt;
- prompt body hash;
- response-contract hash;
- context signature;
- raw-value hash;
- normalizer;
- normalized value;
- response temporary key;
- canonical collection;
- canonical record;
- canonical field or relationship;
- relationship target;
- evidence;
- validation rules;
- validation results;
- project revision;
- commit sequence;
- device timestamp.

Application-created IDs are recorded as application allocations, not falsely attributed to nonexistent response fields.

