An agent-provided filename or hash is a claim.
The application-computed SHA-256 of actual stored bytes is authority.

A required attachment is accepted only when the application verifies:
- expected canonical artifact relationship;
- expected filename or authorized filename pattern;
- media type where contracted;
- actual byte size;
- SHA-256 calculated from actual bytes.

19.2 Custody honesty

Browser-local artifact custody does not imply that:
- an external AI has the bytes;
- an independent reviewer has the bytes;
- a repository runner has the bytes;
- CAD software has the bytes;
- a laboratory has the bytes;
- an external portal has the bytes.

The UI and prompt identify the exact transfer action.

22.4 Native-result authority

Application-native deterministic results are application-owned.
They bypass external response ingestion.
They use a dedicated validated application command.
They do not require human proposal review.

The application atomically records:
- execution-event identity;
- test ID;
- Test IR hash;
- runtime version;
- input artifact IDs;
- input artifact hashes;
- normalized observations;
- normalized result;
- evidence IDs;
- project revision;
- event sequence;
- canonical receipt.

22.5 External deterministic results

Native and external deterministic outcomes may share the canonical deterministicResults collection only when all gate-consumed canonical fields remain consistently application-owned.

External executors submit:
- observations;
- tool metadata;
- evidence claims;
- returned artifacts.

The application validates and normalizes them into canonical application-owned results.
Producer authority does not change by route.

